  "worked_solution": "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=\u0394\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8+4-0 \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{49}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $5\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-5=2\\,m/s.\n$$",
